module bindings.java.spring_response_body_render;

// Spring's `@ResponseBody` is the documented switch for "the return value is
// the response body": no view resolution, the value the method returns is what
// the client receives. `@RestController` is the composed form -- "a convenience
// annotation that is itself annotated with @Controller and @ResponseBody" --
// so it carries the same meaning for every handler in the class. A handler
// that answers with a `@RequestParam` it never escaped is therefore handing
// the request string to whatever renders the response, which is the reflected
// half of an XSS.
//
// Two facts decide the label and both come from the framework's own
// documentation rather than from any one incident:
//
//   - the body annotation: `@ResponseBody` on the method, or `@RestController`
//     on its class. Both are read as annotation tokens, the method's own for
//     the first, the class's for the second -- which is why there are two
//     bindings rather than one, since a call-argument context takes one value
//     per clause and no disjunction.
//   - the declared return type. A bare `String` return is written verbatim by
//     `StringHttpMessageConverter`, so the body is the string; a
//     `ReadableOrder` return is serialized by Jackson to JSON, which is not a
//     render. Without this gate the label lands on every REST handler in an
//     application: on the tree this was measured against, the ungated form
//     added 90 findings of which 84 sat on object-returning JSON endpoints.
//     The handler-visibility clause is the same kind of framework fact --
//     Spring dispatches only public handler methods -- and it keeps the
//     composed annotation from reaching private helpers in the same class.
//
// The sink lands on the return argument itself, so the existing
// `core.HtmlEscape` controls (`escapeHtml4`, `escapeHtml`, `htmlEscape`, ...)
// neutralise the path they actually cover and only that path: a handler that
// escapes one return and leaves another raw reports the raw one, which is the
// correct answer about that handler.
//
// `callee.method` is not the route here because there is no call to name --
// the value is returned, not passed. `callee.analysis ~= "function.return"`
// addresses the return pseudo-call, which is the same route the Flask handler
// bindings use for the identical question in Python.
//
// Note on the matching semantics measured here: on a call-argument context,
// `contains` compares the whole token value, so `contains "ResponseBody"`
// matches `annotation:ResponseBody` and nothing else, while `containsAny`
// matches nothing at all. Spelling the value in full is what makes the clause
// behave as read.

binding springResponseBodyStringRender {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.analysis ~= "function.return" and args.any.context.annotation contains "ResponseBody" and args.any.context.functionModifier contains "public" and args.any.context.returnType contains "String"
  emit sink code.HtmlRender at args[0]
}

binding springRestControllerStringRender {
  query pattern callExpr where callee.analysis ~= "function.return" and args.any.context.classAnnotation contains "RestController" and args.any.context.functionModifier contains "public" and args.any.context.returnType contains "String"
  emit sink code.HtmlRender at args[0]
}
